py_test(name = "discover_holed_inventory_heals_test", srcs = ["discover_holed_inventory_heals_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld:yaml", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"])  # bd qmbp: the reader-visible half of the coverage audit -- a module tracked in the inventory with no node in the graph a reader loads, while freshness reported nothing wrong. Pins that refusing to vouch converges in one pass (the unqueryable symbol resolves) rather than looping, and that re-landing a stale body under a newer inventory cannot silence the hole
py_test(name = "discovery_state_published_graph_test", srcs = ["discovery_state_published_graph_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime"])  # bd wq9i: the inventory<->graph binding itself. ADR 0101 recorded "this run published a graph" as a boolean, which cannot distinguish a seed that landed the *same* bytes at a new inode (must stay an incremental basis) from a foreign body swapped in underneath (must not) -- and no hash diff can see the second, because the inventory still matches the tree. The integration half is discover_unproven_state_forces_full_test; the end-to-end half is weld_worktree_seed_replaced_body_test
py_test(name = "weld_init_policy_exit_test", srcs = ["weld_init_policy_exit_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld:workspace"])  # bd ilax: `wd init` writes two write-once things -- discover.yaml and the managed git policy -- and the exit code answered about the first only, so the documented Mode A -> Mode B upgrade (`rm .weld/.gitignore && wd init --track-graphs`) wrote everything asked for, announced it, and exited 1. Fixing the code alone would have turned a second, silent bug into exit 0: the ignore writer is skip-if-exists too, so --track-graphs over a surviving config-only file writes a .gitattributes declaring a merge policy for artifacts the ignore file still hides. Both halves pinned -- the request that succeeded exits 0, the one that silently did not exits 1 naming the file that blocked it
py_test(name = "graph_identical_body_not_rewritten_test", srcs = ["graph_identical_body_not_rewritten_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld:workspace"])  # bd emyk: the last line of no-change diff bd lrfu left behind. graph.json is written by atomic rename, so an identical-bytes rewrite lands a fresh inode and a fresh st_mtime_ns -- which is the cheap half of the inventory<->graph binding, so published_graph.mtime_ns re-stamped on every no-change discover. Carrying a stale mtime forward is the wrong fix (it costs the steady-state read a full digest, which bd aqqa moved off the graph body); not rewriting an unchanged graph is the only sound one. The skip already existed behind the one caller that passed on_disk_bytes (bd 85tb.2) and now lives in the writer for all of them. Pins both directions -- an identical body holds the inode, a real change is still written -- plus what must NOT be skipped: the volatile sidecar still restamps, and its graph_mtime_ns mirror still pins the untouched file, or every read after a no-change discover falls back to a full parse
py_test(name = "weld_gitignore_probe_test", srcs = ["weld_gitignore_probe_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld:workspace"])  # bd jya6: bd ilax's check one layer out. `wd init --track-graphs` judged Mode B from the managed .weld/.gitignore alone, so a repo whose ROOT .gitignore carries `.weld/` got Mode B declared and none of it applied -- git never saw the artifacts and the warm clone arrived empty, reported as success. Asks git instead of parsing: check-ignore answers for the whole stack (root .gitignore, .git/info/exclude, global core.excludesFile, the managed file) with git's own precedence -- including the rule no parser gets right, that a negation cannot re-include a file under an excluded parent directory -- and names the file, line and pattern. Pins that nothing which worked starts failing: a clean repo, a non-git directory (init is supported there), an already-tracked graph.json under a `.weld/` rule (git commits a tracked file regardless, so that repo IS in Mode B), and bare `wd init`, which requested no mode
py_test(name = "weld_config_file_strategy_test", srcs = ["weld_config_file_strategy_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"])  # bd q85a: config_file could only be pointed at an explicit files: list, so covering a directory meant enumerating it -- the lesson bd crau already paid for, where a hand-listed set leaves the NEXT file invisible. discover.yaml's own resolver has always accepted glob/path/files interchangeably, so a glob entry was already resolved for incremental hashing and ADR 0101 coverage while the strategy read none of it. The load-bearing pin is the last test: the set the strategy emits must equal the set resolve_source_files records, or the difference reads as permanently uncovered scope -- staleness that never clears and a full discovery on every read
py_test(name = "discover_yaml_shipped_data_coverage_test", srcs = ["discover_yaml_shipped_data_coverage_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld:contract", "//weld:yaml", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"], tags = ["external"])  # bd q85a: fourth of the discover_yaml_*_coverage family, same shape and same reason as the python/bazel/tool_script three -- asserts on the host repo's .weld/discover.yaml plus the tree, neither of which Bazel sees as an input, so ``external`` keeps a config-only edit from replaying a cached PASS. weld/languages/*.yaml, weld/templates/* and weld/docs/*.md ship in the wheel and are named in BUILD filegroups, so the build system knew they existed and the graph did not. ADR 0111 rules out minting them from the BUILD glob (srcs is a referrer, not a discovery scope; stubs would inflate ADR 0101 coverage with files discovery never read), so each family needs a real source entry. Pins the node-type decision per family -- config for data weld reads at runtime, doc for the documentation it ships, neither needing a new VALID_NODE_TYPES entry -- and that the markdown id_prefix mirrors its directory, without which //weld/docs:docs resolves nothing and the gap only looks closed
py_test(name = "discovery_state_no_timestamp_test", srcs = ["discovery_state_no_timestamp_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime"])  # bd lrfu: the inventory records claims, not a clock. ADR 0110 tracks this file, so the created_at save_state stamped on every write became a commit-worthy diff on a discover that found nothing changed. Dropped rather than preserved -- ADR 0065 already keeps volatile per-run values in the gitignored graph-meta.json, which carries the same wall clock as updated_at. Pins byte-stability of the write path (not merely the key's absence), that a real inventory change still moves the file, and that a legacy payload carrying created_at still loads as an incremental basis -- dropping the key is deliberately not a STATE_VERSION bump, which would cost every repo a full re-discovery
py_test(name = "discovery_state_concurrent_save_test", srcs = ["discovery_state_concurrent_save_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime"])  # bd 70he: save_state used a FIXED temp path (state_path.with_suffix(".tmp")) before its atomic rename, so two concurrent discover(live_root, incremental=False) callers against the same root -- weld_graph_integrity_regression_test and its BUILD-macro sibling weld_artifact_class_regression_test are exactly such a pair -- could interleave so the second rename targeted a temp file the first had already consumed, raising an uncaught FileNotFoundError out of discover() (ERROR, not FAIL; matches "passed in isolation" far better than a deterministic stale-read would). Fixed by routing through weld.workspace_state.atomic_write_text, which already solves this elsewhere via tempfile.mkstemp's collision-proof naming. Reproduces the exact interleaving deterministically via a threading.Event-paced os.replace patch (Path.replace calls os.replace internally, so the hook is stable across both the old and new temp-naming implementation) rather than relying on real thread-scheduling luck
py_test(name = "incremental_rel_path_form_test", srcs = ["incremental_rel_path_form_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime"])  # bd pbi8: the index<->graph path-form contract. The stale set is spelled by weld (OS-native str()), props.file/provenance.file by ~40 strategies (half as_posix(), half str()) -- byte-identical on POSIX, divergent off it, where ADR 0074 misfires in both tiers and the provenance tier retains an attributable edge *unconditionally* (silent staleness, strictly worse than the endpoint floor it replaced). Weld has no Windows lane, so the platform is simulated by patching weld._rel_path._FOREIGN_SEPARATORS to the tuple it would compute there; the last class runs unsimulated and pins that a literal-backslash POSIX filename is NOT folded, which is what separates this from the unconditional replace the read-side query/lint paths take
py_test(name = "weld_rel_to_root_test", srcs = ["weld_rel_to_root_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies"])  # bd v552: the other half of the split pbi8 folded around. The index vocabulary (_source_resolve -> DiscoveryState.files -> dirty/stale) was OS-native while glob_match spelled its in-scope listing as_posix(), so off POSIX files_missing_from_inventory compared POSIX against native, every in-scope file read as uncovered, and auto-refresh re-ran discovery on every read. Both ends had to move together -- the dirty set flows on as IncrementalHint.dirty_files and is matched against a path the strategy re-derives -- so the fix is a construction helper (weld._rel_path.rel_to_root) used at both. Unlike the pbi8 fold, this is not a string operation and cannot be simulated by patching _FOREIGN_SEPARATORS: the non-POSIX platform is supplied as PureWindowsPath input instead. The last class runs on real POSIX paths and pins that a file legitimately named weird\\name.py is NOT folded
py_test(name = "weld_graph_path_canonicalization_test", srcs = ["weld_graph_path_canonicalization_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime"])  # bd 244j: the stored artifact's half. Node ids have been POSIX since ADR 0041 but props.file / props.declared_in / props.provenance.file were left to whichever strategy wrote them, ~half as_posix() and ~half str(), so off POSIX one graph carries both spellings for sibling files and every read-side consumer solved it locally and inconsistently. Canonicalized once in _discover_postprocess.post_process, the boundary both discover paths funnel through, which is what preserves incremental==full byte-identity. The pass is skipped outright when the platform separator is already canonical, so on POSIX no stored byte moves and this lands with no migration (ADR 0065, ADR 0012 §3) -- the unsimulated class is the pin for that, and for the literal-backslash POSIX filename a write-side fold must not claim. bd mzv1 adds props.dir, the fourth path-shaped prop, left out of the first pass because no reader matched on it -- which made an artifact with canonical file anchors and native directory anchors, the one state worse than either spelling used consistently. bd 3x85 is the read side: impact_surfaces._normalize_path now delegates its separator fold to _rel_path.canonical_rel_path (identity on POSIX, so a file legitimately named a\\b.py stops being misread), while _graph_strategy_pair deliberately keeps the unconditional form -- it feeds a gate-failing lint, where losing the tolerance turns a pre-244j off-POSIX graph into a false-positive storm that blocks somebody. ReadSideFoldFollowsThePlatformTest pins both sides of that split so the survivor does not read as an oversight
py_test(name = "discover_failed_strategy_repairs_test", srcs = ["discover_failed_strategy_repairs_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld:yaml", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"])  # bd hch4: the reader-visible half. files_with_no_nodes was the unconditional complement of the graph's anchors, so the run that FAILED a file wrote the record saying its strategy had DECIDED nothing belongs there -- and since that record keys on the path alone, a failure caused by anything but the file's own bytes never re-armed. Pins that a refused strategy repairs on the first pass that can run it, that a genuinely declined file is still exempt (or the fix would trade a silent hole for a permanent slow path), and that a named hole still leaves an incremental basis
py_test(name = "discovery_state_failed_strategy_test", srcs = ["discovery_state_failed_strategy_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies"])  # bd hch4: the contract under the test above -- the exemption matrix (both sets exempt the vouching audit; only a decision exempts the ADR 0008 per-file repair), the reporting channel, and how a state written before the split is read after it
py_test(name = "discover_failed_source_repairs_test", srcs = ["discover_failed_source_repairs_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld:yaml", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"])  # bd um00: the entry-keyed sibling of hch4's file-keyed repair, over a real discover(). A command-only external_json source resolves no files at all, so the file-keyed channel (and the ADR 0008 per-file repair it arms) never sees it -- confirmed empirically that such a source runs exactly once, on the first full discovery, and never again while the incremental basis holds, success or failure alike. Pins that a failure is recorded by entry fingerprint with a kind and bounded reason, that wd doctor reports it, that the NEXT discover retries the command even though nothing tracked on disk changed (the re-arm proof, not just the recording), that two failures in a row still leave the third pass able to repair, that a recorded entry-level failure never touches the vouching audit, and (the control) that a source WITH a file footprint keeps using the file-keyed channel only
py_test(name = "discovery_state_failed_source_test", srcs = ["discovery_state_failed_source_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies"])  # bd um00: the contract under the test above -- entry_fingerprint stability (content-sensitive, reorder-invariant, so a sources: reorder cannot orphan a record), the note/drain reporting channel (overwrite-per-entry-id, bounded reason), sources_needing_retry's set logic (a stale id for an entry no longer present is never forced, because a config edit already forces a full run through the whole-config fingerprint), state round-tripping including an older weld's state and malformed values, and that save_state_for_graph threads source_failed straight through with no graph-anchoring bound (unlike strategy_failed, a footprint-less entry has no file to test anchoring against)
py_test(name = "weld_doctor_failed_sources_test", srcs = ["weld_doctor_failed_sources_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime"])  # bd um00: wd doctor is the chosen surface for sources_with_failed_strategy -- not coverage_stale / wd stale, matching bd 0jck's reasoning for the file-keyed sibling (a permanently failing command would otherwise earn a refresh, and re-spawn the subprocess, on every read, forever) and bd 4625's closed stale_sources vocabulary having no path-shaped slot for a footprint-less entry. Split into its own file rather than grown onto weld_doctor_test.py, which was already near the line-count cap, matching how every other doctor concern already lives in its own file
