[py_test(name = _n, srcs = ["_import_table_fixture.py", _n + ".py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies"]) for _n in ("weld_python_callgraph_sibling_import_test", "weld_python_callgraph_sibling_import_refusals_test", "weld_python_callgraph_relative_import_test", "weld_python_callgraph_relative_import_refusals_test")]  # bd zr486: the explicit half of the same family -- `from .helper import work` in pkg/caller.py recorded ("helper", "work") because _build_import_table dropped node.level, so a bare work() resolved to symbol:py:helper:work, a module existing under no spelling, minted origin=external, while pkg/helper.py collected no caller at all; `from . import x` (node.module is None) was skipped outright, so that call fell to the sentinel. Measured on a package seed: three fabricated external nodes, two sentinels, five call edges all pointing somewhere other than the definition meant. Unlike sigz2 this half is arithmetic, not inference -- level is written in the source -- so there is no glob_modules bound to earn and the refusals are CPython's own sanity checks rather than a calibration: beyond the top-level package, and no parent package at all. Both refusal cases assert the sentinel AND the absence of a minted id, because at the tree root the fabricated name and a real sibling coincide, which is the shape in which a wrong rule passes review. Runs through close_graph for the same reason the sigz2 pair does (the resolved module travels on props.import_attr into weld._graph_closure_import_attr); hence the //weld:runtime dep. Shares the harness, which is why it is named for the import table rather than for either half. bd sigz2: a bare module name inside a directory with no __init__.py binds against that directory, so `from lint_test_hygiene import changed_test_lines` in tools/lint_pinned_citations.py means tools.lint_test_hygiene -- the strategy read the spelling literally and emitted a confident edge to symbol:py:lint_test_hygiene:changed_test_lines, a module that exists under no spelling, so the real definition collected no caller and wd callers on the primitive behind six diff-scoped lints answered 1 of 3. Both refusals get a shape live in this repo rather than an invented one: weld/providers/anthropic.py opens `from anthropic import Anthropic` beside an __init__.py (a package, so the name is the third-party SDK and a sibling-first rule would resolve the module to ITSELF), and a script directory importing a third-party name with no file behind it must keep the literal spelling. The reported topology -- one primitive, three callers, two of them cross-module -- is its own case, because a one-consumer fixture cannot reproduce a caller COUNT. Runs extract through close_graph as well as alone, since the corrected module travels on props.import_attr into weld._graph_closure_import_attr and a hint naming the fabricated module would send every closure rule looking under a module that is not there; hence the //weld:runtime dep.
[py_test(name = _n, srcs = ["_import_table_fixture.py", _n + ".py"], deps = ["//weld/strategies"]) for _n in ("weld_python_callgraph_lazy_api_test", "weld_python_callgraph_lazy_api_refusals_test", "weld_python_callgraph_source_root_import_test")]  # bd 80zz3: the third consumer of the import-table harness, and the first that reads the table back rather than correcting it. This repo defers an import into a function on purpose (to keep a symbol-id or import-table shape defined once while dodging a circular import) and unpacks the accessor's return into locals -- `_symbol_id, build_import_table = _callgraph_api()` then `build_import_table(...)`. The report separated two mechanisms and only one was broken: `_build_import_table` walks the whole module, so a function-scoped `from ... import` was ALREADY a table entry and a call by the imported name already resolved (pinned here, because the alias rule resolves THROUGH that entry and a later narrowing of the table to module-level imports would silently take both halves away); the local name bound by the unpack is in no table, so it fell to a sentinel and `wd callers` on python_callgraph's own contract surface answered 1 of 2 -- complete-looking and wrong, on exactly the module whose signature was about to change. Measured over this repo the rule fires on 14 call sites and moves 7 edges, sentinel -> definition, and NOTHING else: the same extract with the accessor table forced empty differs by those 7 and no more. The refusals file is the load-bearing half, because reading a function's return value is the move that fabricates an edge whenever the value is not written in the source -- the sigz2/zr486 class. Three bounds hold it there: the accessor is a module-level, undecorated, once-bound `def` whose whole body is imports plus one return of names those imports bound; each returned name is bound by exactly ONE import in the module, and it is the accessor's own (a module-global the accessor merely closes over is refused -- a deliberate under-report, since proving it needs proof that nothing rebinds it and a module-level import resolves directly anyway); and the local name is bound once in its own scope, counted with a deliberately unbounded walk so a miscount can only refuse more. Every refusal asserts the caller SET of the declined definition, not just the absent edge -- that set is literally what `wd callers` renders, and unlike zr486's "no such id was minted" it keeps its bite when the declined target is a real symbol the same tree defines, which here it always is. No //weld:runtime dep: alias resolution completes inside the strategy and mints no props.import_attr hint for the closure to move. bd z98p7 / ADR 0143 (field-eval M2): the fourth consumer of the import-table harness, and the generalization of sigz2 above -- a bare name binding against the importer's own non-package directory is the narrow end of "which directory does Python put on sys.path", and the wide end is a file INSIDE a package tree, whose sys.path entry is the first ancestor without an __init__.py. Under a src/ layout every first-party import is that shape: src/acme/config.py is keyed src.acme.config by its path and written acme.config by every import in the tree, so the literal reading minted a speculative twin, put both call edges on it, and left wd callers on the node that holds the file answering "no callers" -- 112 dual-identity symbols in one service the evaluator measured, and a byte-identical two-layout experiment where the root layout found callers and the src/ one found none. The rewrite is bounded by the same glob_modules admission the narrow end earns (ADR 0074), so an over-strip can only name a module that does not exist and decline. Two refusals get their own case because the wider reach is what makes them necessary: a literal spelling the glob already owns wins over the inference, and a candidate equal to the importing file's own module is refused (a package __init__ saying `import pkg`, which the other two refusals both pass). A third case pins what is deliberately NOT a refusal: sys.path[0] precedence holds over the standard library one level up exactly as sigz2 already pins it beside the importer -- ADR 0143 D3 lists a stdlib guard, but that bullet restates the guard the generalization removes, and implementing it would reverse a shipped contract test while the same decision claims the shipped rewrites are made identically. Two more pin the walk itself: it stops at the repository root rather than probing above a tree whose own root is a package, and it drops repo-relative segments in lockstep with the directories it probes, so a file whose STEM contains a dot (demo.launch.py, named ...launch.demo.launch) cannot skew the prefix one level deep and resolve its import to itself. No //weld:runtime dep: every assertion reads what extract wrote, and no props.import_attr hint is minted for the closure to move.
