[py_test(name = _n, srcs = ["_graph_closure_import_attr_fixture.py", _n + ".py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies"]) for _n in ("weld_graph_closure_import_attr_test", "weld_graph_closure_import_attr_undo_test", "weld_graph_closure_class_base_test")]  # bd yhz70: `from PARENT import CHILD` + `CHILD.attr()` is two readings of one syntax, and python_callgraph proved modulehood from the set it also tags origins with -- which ADR 0074 derives per-glob on a full discover and as the cross-glob prior-node union on an incremental one, so a caller in one glob calling into another resolved symbol:py:lib.inner:work after a refresh and fell to symbol:unresolved:work on a full run of the same tree. Not a recall difference: an incremental graph a full discover never produces. The strategy now decides only what its own glob owns (the same question on both paths) and records the two names the reading turns on; weld._graph_closure_import_attr decides the rest once, on the merged graph. Split resolution from undo for the same reason the reexport trio next door is split: the refusals are what keep a rule that assembles a node id out of stored strings safer than the sentinel it replaces (a value base, a stdlib value import, any hint whose fields cannot be vouched for), while the undo is a separate mechanism with its own failure mode -- this is the second closure rule to move an endpoint on a RETAINED edge, and an incremental round never re-walks a clean caller, so deleting the definer has to restore and re-derive rather than inherit. It needs no bookkeeping key to do it, unlike the reexport retarget: the endpoint it replaced is symbol:unresolved:<attr>, a pure function of the hint the edge still carries. The class-base reading (bd vrdcj) is the third file: `from weld.bm25 import BM25Corpus` + `BM25Corpus.from_nodes()` names the method's own symbol, which the walk already emitted under its dotted qualname -- so unlike the submodule rule it mints nothing, and both halves of its proof (the base is a walked kind=class, the method under it is a walked symbol) get a case refusing alone. Measured at fix time: 78 hinted edges, 3 with a definite class base and a definite method, 32 whose base is a module-level constant -- a dict, a compiled regex, a message template -- so resolving on the base alone would have fabricated a bigger population than the deferral removed  # A call whose call site imports the symbol from a re-export facade used to land on a speculative stub for a module that defines nothing (`symbol:py:weld.contract:validate_graph`), so `wd callers` on the real definition answered "no callers" for every consumer that took the documented public import path -- the blast radius of a change to a re-exported symbol read as empty. The closure now follows the facade's own imports to the module that defines the name. Split three ways, along the seam the code itself splits on -- what resolves (weld._graph_closure_reexport), what it refuses, and the edge surgery that keeps a moved endpoint re-derivable (weld._graph_closure_reexport_edges). Resolution vs. guards for the same reason the N4 pair above is: `imports_from` records modules, not the names they bind, so the retarget is an inference, and the guards are what keep it safer than the stub it replaces -- it must not guess between two imported modules that both define the name, must not walk an import cycle or an unbounded chain, and must never touch a stdlib or external stub, where there is no import table to follow. The guards file also owns the idempotence half: this is the only closure rule that mutates a RETAINED edge rather than re-deriving from node props, so it strips its own prior output (props.reexport_to / reexport_from) and re-mints the stub before re-deriving, which is what keeps incremental == full when the definition later moves or disappears  # field-eval N4: the closure resolves a first-party Python import against the importer's ancestor directories instead of minting an external package node beside the file the graph already holds (1441 such nodes in this repo). Split into resolution vs. guards because the guards are what make the fix safe rather than a worse defect: the acme.platform.* families must still mint (that is the cross-repo recall win the regression rode in on), a stdlib name must not be captured by a same-named sibling -- this repo really has weld/warnings.py and weld/trace.py -- and an inferred name must land on a real file, never on a speculative symbol stub that merely shares a module name
